AB  - Policy-constrained generative AI in financial services sits at the intersection of two regulatory regimes that disagree on almost everything except cost of failure: a model can be plausibly correct and yet violate Know Your Customer (KYC), suitability, fair-lending, or recordkeeping obligations in ways that scale with throughput. Existing safety layers, whether prompt-engineered guardrails or static keyword filters, neither prove compliance nor leave the audit trail an examiner will eventually ask for. We present PolicyAgent, a five-agent generative AI framework that couples retrieval over a structured policy corpus with formal automated reasoning checks and tamper-evident audit. A Policy Ingestion and Indexing Agent (PIIA) compiles institutional policy documents into a typed reasoning graph; a Reasoning Verification Agent (RVA) discharges per-response obligations through an Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) automated-reasoning backend; a Generative Drafting Agent (GDA), built on a 13B Code-Llama variant fine-tuned with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), produces responses constrained by the RVA feasibility envelope; an Audit Trail Orchestrator (ATO) records every action in a SHA-256 chained, append-only log; and a Continuous Policy Evaluation Coordinator (CPEC) runs the FinPolicyBench evaluation harness against the live system. Evaluated on FinPolicyBench, a reproducible benchmark of 47,200 policy-constrained queries spanning seven obligation categories across a 90-day controlled Continuous Integration / Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline, PolicyAgent achieves a policy-compliance F1 of 0.926, a 71.3% reduction in policy violations per 1,000 responses relative to a prompt-guardrail baseline, a mean reasoning-discharge latency of 412 ms, and a Policy Compliance Score Pi = 0.918, with full ablation studies confirming each agent contributes independently. All quantitative results are simulation-derived: the evaluation uses synthetic and public-text inputs in a controlled CI/CD simulation, with no live customer data and no production deployment; live-deployment validation remains the primary open problem.
ER  - 

