AB  - Open source software relies on volunteer contributors, yet onboarding newcomers remains difficult and practices for ensuring quality are fragmented. Existing continuous integration pipelines, linters, templates, and documentation rarely adapt to repository-specific norms or explain failures in actionable terms, producing low-quality pull requests, prolonged reviews, and contributor attrition. This paper presents ContextPilot, a modular, multi-agent framework that provides guidance grounded in project artifacts. ContextPilot ingests repository metadata-such as readme files, contribution guidelines, workflow definitions, and linter and test configurations-into a retrieval index. Specialized agents for repositories, feedback, editing environments, and conversation coordinate with a large language model to transform logs, code differences, and policies into human-readable recommendations. We detail the architecture, data schema, prompting strategies, and safeguards. A prototype integrates with GitHub webhooks and a Visual Studio Code extension. Illustrative scenarios show how ContextPilot explains pipeline failures, offers proactive guidance during local development, and reuses organizational knowledge from prior contributions to reduce repeated maintainer effort. We outline an evaluation plan comparing standard workflows with ContextPilot-augmented workflows across efficiency, contribution quality, maintainer workload, contributor retention, and scalability, and we discuss threats to validity. ContextPilot turns quality enforcement from a gatekeeping step into an instructive experience.
ER  - 

