AB  - Power grid software used in dispatching, substation monitoring, metering, and distribution management must satisfy stronger safety, traceability, and release-governance requirements than generic enterprise applications. Although recent studies on Large Language Model (lLM)-assisted code review and automated test generation have improved developer productivity, their direct adoption in grid software remains limited by weak domain grounding, inadequate risk prioritization, and insufficient coupling between review evidence and Continuous Integration (CI) / Continuous Delivery (CD) execution. This paper proposes a lifecycle-aware intelligent engineering framework for power grid software systems. A bounded re-trieval-augmented semantic review module first extracts structured change intent from commits, requirement notes, and defect tickets, then retrieves grid rules, historical incidents, and CI evidence, and finally invokes a locally deployed code LLM only within a controlled context window to produce evidence-linked review comments and test intents. A multi-source risk model then integrates static severity, historical defect density, semantic change scope, business criticality, and test-gap signals to allocate validation budget and trigger staged execution. Generated tests are retained only after deterministic compilation, oracle checking, and sandbox validation, while high-risk changes additionally activate digital-twin replay. Experiments on $\mathbf{1 8 6}$ change sets from 12 grid-related repositories show that precision improves from 0.74 to 0.87, recall from 0.68 to 0.82, and effective coverage from 11% to 29%, while median review time is reduced by 34%. Paired statistical tests confirm that the improvements in precision, recall, and effective coverage are significant ($\mathbf{p}\lt0.01$).
ER  - 

