AB  - Software supply-chain attacks and latent vulnerabilities increasingly arise not from isolated code defects, but from complex interactions across software evolution artifacts such as commits, dependency updates, configuration changes, and continuous integration pipelines. Existing AI-based vulnerability detection approaches largely operate on static code snapshots or treat software artifacts independently, limiting their effectiveness against modern, temporally evolving security threats. This paper proposes TRACESEC, a trace-aware neuro-symbolic framework for vulnerability detection and supply-chain security analysis that models vulnerabilities as causal narratives emerging over time. TRACESEC constructs a Temporal Software Trace Graph that jointly captures code changes, dependency relationships, commit histories, and build signals across software versions. A hybrid reasoning engine integrates graph neural networks and large language model embeddings with symbolic security constraints derived from standardized vulnerability taxonomies. Unlike prior black-box approaches, TRACESEC produces explainable vulnerability predictions grounded in real software evolution traces, enabling early detection of supply-chain compromise and actionable developer insights. The framework is evaluated using exclusively publicly available datasets, demonstrating improved detection accuracy and earlier identification of vulnerability emergence compared to static and LLM-only baselines.
ER  - 

