AB  - Most university cybersecurity education is designed and implemented based on various well-established frameworks like CSEC2017 and the NICE Workforce Framework. While these standards are good to begin with, they are neither flexible nor provide practical exposure to rapidly changing threat landscapes. This paper, on the other hand, presents a modular, scenario-based cybersecurity curriculum that amalgamates manually curated real-time threat intelligence to develop human analytical capabilities instead of building artificial intelligence or machine learning models. The proposed system integrated theoretical modules, practical simulations, and scenario-driven learning environments through Streamlit-based application. A dynamic library of threat scenarios will be mapped against specific job roles and skill requirements; hands-on simulation labs, using open-source tools such as Metasploit, Snort, Wireshark, and Kali Linux, will enable learners to analyze and mitigate threats in real time. This allows the content administrators and instructors to update the content on a role-based, securely designed interface with SQLite data management and SHA-256 authentication. Academic and industry expert-led quarterly workshops ensure the continuous integration of emergent threat intelligence and region-specific challenges. By focusing on manual reasoning, problem-solving, and situational awareness, the proposed system closes the gap between academic training and industry readiness for future cybersecurity professionals to respond effectively to evolving digital threats.
ER  - 

